From: pulpoff <pashkovsky@gmail.com>
Subject: The AOD uses the panel's own clock where it has one (de.yesman.neo aod)

The S10e's S6E3FA7 can draw analog hands over its frame memory and tick them by itself in its low power
mode, as Samsung's AOD does: the phone then sleeps for hours with the time right. With the panel's
self_clock and lpm attributes writable (a udev rule gives them to the video group), the AOD draws a dim
dial instead of the time and, once faded in, puts the panel into low power mode (10 nits) and starts its
hands at the dial's centre, so the content no longer walks every minute (0117 moves it every two hours
instead). Fading out, the panel leaves low power mode first. The writes go through one worker thread,
in order, since the panel takes up to a few hundred ms. A refused clock (no hand image, a rotated
panel) shows the time as before.
